Reproducibility

result &lt;- sites |&gt;
  sf::st_buffer(dist = 500) |&gt;
  sf::st_intersection(wetlands) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(area = sum(sf::st_area(geometry)))

Loops/re-use

process_catchment &lt;- function(catchment, rivers, sites) {
  rivers |&gt;
    sf::st_intersection(catchment) |&gt;
    sf::st_join(sites)
}
results &lt;- purrr::map(
  catchments,
  process_catchment,
  rivers = rivers,
  sites = sites
)

Notes on running GLM

GLM does not have the capability to see the png output. Therefore, it asked a series of questions about what it looked like. This would align with the standard workflow of having a basic idea of what the plot looks like. It does preclude using a map that you like as template to replicate.

Option1: sit-back and absorb…🪱 tapeworm strategy
Option2: run in parallel…🐒 monkey strategy?

this strategy is better if you are familiar in Rstudio
If you do the later, we will be curious to see your outputs (e.g. how it goes for you)




